WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:04.720
ARMAN RYE: Hi there.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.530
My name is Arman Rye, and
I'm a networking specialist

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.910
at Google Cloud.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.730
In recent years, there have
been record-breaking numbers

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.490
of DDoS, distributed
denial of service,

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.630
attacks against organizations
and networks of many sizes,

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.050
costing companies major
financial losses, drops

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.690
in production, and
security threats.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.690
As adversaries continue to
expand their capabilities

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.720
and frequency of
attacks, any organization

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.270
hosting a web service
via the public internet

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.460
is susceptible to attack.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.190
While it isn't realistic
to prevent all attacks,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.090
it's essential to mitigate
the impact when it happens.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.470
Cloud Armor is Google Cloud's
DDoS defense service and web

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.090
application firewall solution.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.780
One of the features available
as part of Cloud Armor

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.910
is Adaptive Protection.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.670
Adaptive Protection helps
you protect your Google Cloud

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.530
applications, websites, and
services against layer 7 DDoS

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.940
attacks, such as HTTP floods
and other high-frequency layer

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.450
7, application-level,
malicious activity.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.450
Adaptive Protection is a
part of Cloud Armor, which

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.210
is a broader network
security service that

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.800
provides defenses against
DDoS and application attacks.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.860
Adaptive Protection builds
machine learning models

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.090
to identify and block web
attacks on your infrastructure.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.780
It does this by first learning
what your normal traffic

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.900
pattern looks like for each
internet-facing website

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.500
or service, which might take
an hour to a couple of days

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.140
in order to give it enough
time to train the model.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.410
This includes several dozen
features and characteristics

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.120
of incoming requests,
for example,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.060
the source of the
traffic or elements

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.980
of the HTTP request coming in.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.990
Once it's done learning
the baseline traffic

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.240
patterns of a web
service, it will then

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:59.350
shift to actively monitoring
for layer 7 DDoS attacks.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.640
And it continues
to learn over time,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.690
so as the traffic patterns
of good requests change,

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:07.870
it adjusts its
models automatically.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.120
If it detects an
anomalous deviation

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.740
from your normal
traffic pattern,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.890
it will alert you on
this anomalous activity.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.860
It then generates a signature
describing the potential attack

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.270
and generates a custom Google
Cloud Armor web application

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.280
firewall rule to
block the signature.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.590
Now I'll show you how it works.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.160
I've set up a simple managed
instance group sitting

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.630
in us-east-1.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.530
It's a backend service sitting
behind a global HTTPS load

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:36.790
balancer.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.010
I've also configured
a Cloud Armor security

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.710
policy with Adaptive
Protection turned on.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.680
Using a script, I've
simulated our good,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.350
normal traffic with a
20-request-per-second range

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.410
and left it on for one hour.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:54.200
This traffic was used
for Adaptive Protection

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.460
to profile our normal
traffic pattern.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.520
After an hour, I will simulate
malicious user traffic

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.280
to trigger Adaptive Protection's
detection in real time.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.600
Here we are in the Google Cloud
UI on the Cloud Armor page.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.230
This is where you manage all
of your Cloud Armor security

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:12.610
policies.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.830
Listed are multiple
security policies

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.990
that I have in this project.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.780
Clicking in on
gcp-demo-policy, you

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.900
can see a handful of
example rules are listed.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.240
The default action
is Allow, which

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.070
means that if no
rules are triggered,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:29.620
traffic will get sent to
an instance group sitting

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.630
in us-east-1.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.700
In addition to the
handful of rules,

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:38.600
Adaptive Protection is enabled
for this Cloud Armor policy.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.970
Selecting the
Targets tab, you can

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.930
see that this policy is applied
to the unmanaged-web-backend

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.490
backend service.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.380
This is the backend
service of the load

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.330
balancer, where the
instance groups are

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:51.560
located for the demo.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.340
Under the Adaptive
Protection tab,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.150
you can see that there has
been a steady flow of traffic

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:02.960
to my backend instances for
the last one hour at about

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.580
22 requests per second.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.660
This traffic is hitting
the unmanaged-web-backend,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:12.950
which is what I applied my
Cloud Armor security policy to.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.170
This security policy has
Adaptive Protection enabled,

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.880
so this traffic has now been
profiled by Adaptive Protection

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.640
to indicate a normal
traffic pattern.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.580
In order to simulate
abnormal traffic behavior,

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:29.840
I created a script to send
around 2,000 HTTPS requests

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.400
per second to the public IP
address of my L7 global load

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.810
balancer that is front-ending
my us-east-1 managed instance

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.230
group on Google Cloud.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:44.140
After several minutes have
passed from my script running,

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.290
I'll head back to the
Adaptive Protection tab.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:50.290
Here you can see a spike in
the number of allowed requests,

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:54.040
from around 22 requests
per second to over 2,000

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.170
requests per second.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.270
You can see under
Potential Attacks

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:01.060
that Cloud Armor has flagged
this simulated traffic

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.190
as a potential attack.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:06.070
Drilling into the most
recent alert, the Signature

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:10.480
and Recommended Rule tells us
the confidence level of 97%

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.310
that it is an attack.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.380
Looking into the
details, you can

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.740
see the RemoteHost IP is
listed as part of the attack

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.290
signature, and there
is a recommended rule

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:24.880
to update my gcp-demo-policy
security policy to protect

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.400
against this attack.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.410
Right from here, I
can apply the rule

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.020
in preview mode
or blocking mode.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.690
Preview mode lets you
preview the effects of a rule

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:38.410
without enforcing it, while
blocking mode enforces the rule

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.420
and starts blocking
traffic right away.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.820
The attack confidence
and recommended rule

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.730
will dictate whether you
should deploy in a preview

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:51.370
or blocking mode and varies
from application to application.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:55.030
I'm going to select
preview mode.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:59.230
Now we'll head back to my
existing Cloud Armor policy.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.740
Here you can see a new
rule with priority 59

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.810
has been created
in a preview mode

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.960
within minutes of the
attack being kicked off.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:11.200
When a DDoS attack happens,
it's a race against the clock

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.180
to minimize any damage.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.910
Cloud Armor's Adaptive
Protection drastically

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.250
reduces the time that
incident responders

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:21.220
have to spend performing
analysis and proposing

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:24.730
mitigations to block
the ongoing attack.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.400
You get actionable
insights right away

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:31.060
via machine learning-powered
analysis, pattern detection,

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.340
and recommended mitigations.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:36.250
Adaptive Protection is part
of the Cloud Armor Managed

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.470
